p(26,10,"This could not go on; no horse could keep his footing under such circumstances",
 "Black Beauty falls on both knees from pain. Smith is thrown off and does not get up. The horse stands in pain and listens for help.",
 "This could not go on. No horse could keep his feet under such a strain, because the pain was too great. I fell hard on both knees. Smith was thrown off by my fall, and because of my speed he must have fallen with great force. I soon got up and limped to the side of the road, where there were no stones. The moon had just come up over the hedge, and by its light I could see Smith lying a few yards from me. He did not get up. He tried a little to do so, and then there was a heavy groan. I could have groaned too, because I was in great pain in my foot and knees. But horses are used to bearing their pain without a sound, so I made no sound. I stood there and listened. I heard one more heavy groan from Smith. He now lay in full moonlight, but I could see no movement. I could do nothing for him or for myself, but how I listened for the sound of a horse, or wheels, or steps! Not many people used the road, and at that time of night we might wait for hours before help came. I stood watching and listening. It was a calm, sweet April night. There were no sounds except a few low notes of a nightingale, and nothing moved except the white clouds near the moon and a brown owl that flew quickly over the hedge. It made me think of the summer nights long ago, when I used to lie beside my mother in the green, pleasant meadow at Farmer Grey’s.",
 ["footing","circumstances","limped","intense","uttered","frequented","nightingale","owl","flitted"])
